## The 4 Prong Issues

Each issue has a specific timeline and a specific fix. All 4 are preventable at the buying stage.

---

### Issue 1 — Shared Prong Drift

An eternity band uses shared prongs to fit more stones in a small space. One prong tip grips the right edge of one stone and the left edge of the next. That shared tip is the weakest point in the band.

Under daily men's wear, that tip wears unevenly. The stone that absorbs more lateral force pushes the prong tip in one direction. The adjacent stone loses grip first.

By year 3–5, two adjacent stones are loose simultaneously.

The fix at buying: choose channel-set over shared prong for daily wear. The [Geometric Channel at $1,410](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/geometric-channel-mens-diamond-wedding-band-in-14k-white-gold-650mm-by-james-allen-item-305349?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) has no shared prongs. The [Channel Set Satin Finish in Platinum at $2,680](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/channel-set-satin-finish-mens-diamond-wedding-band-in-platinum-650mm-by-james-allen-item-304396?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses platinum channel walls.

If you want shared prong, insist on milgrain borders — see [Issue 4](#issue-4) below.

---

### Issue 2 — Channel Wall Undercutting

A channel-set eternity band presses two metal walls over the diamond girdle by machine. The walls hold the stone between them. The holding force depends on wall thickness.

Under lateral impact — which men's hands take constantly — the channel wall gradually compresses. If the wall is under 0.5mm at the girdle seat, it cracks. A cracked channel wall means the stone moves inward.

It won't fall out immediately, but it will wobble.

The fix: platinum channel walls are harder (160 HV) than 14k gold (155 HV). They resist compression longer. For channel-set, platinum is the right metal choice over a 20-year horizon.

The [Channel Set Satin Finish in Platinum at $2,680](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/channel-set-satin-finish-mens-diamond-wedding-band-in-platinum-650mm-by-james-allen-item-304396?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) has verified platinum channel construction.

---

### Issue 3 — Full Eternity Rotation Wear

A full eternity band that fits slightly loose rotates on the finger. It's a 360° band — there's no orientation marker to tell you which way it's sitting. The rotation is constant and unnoticed.

The problem: the top-of-finger position absorbs daily impact — table edges, doorknobs, gym equipment. That quadrant of the band gets 3–5× more impact than the palm-side settings. After 5 years, the 12 o'clock settings show wear that the 6 o'clock settings don't.

The fix: order a full eternity band 1/4 to 1/2 size larger than your ring size. This reduces rotation. Add milgrain borders — they act as low-friction edge markers that slow rotation on skin.

The [Milgrain Vertical Row Eternity at $2,880](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/milgrain-vertical-row-diamond-eternity-ring-in-14k-white-gold-7-mm-3-8-ct-tw-item-288422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) has milgrain on both edges. See the [men's diamond wedding band guide](/mens-diamond-wedding-band) for the full ring size to fit relationship.

---

### Issue 4 — 14k White Gold Prong Tip Softening

14k white gold is naturally yellowish. Rhodium plating creates the white finish. That plating is 0.5–2.5 microns thick.

At prong tips — the highest-contact area — rhodium wears off in 18–24 months of daily use.

After the rhodium is gone, bare 14k gold is exposed at the prong tip. 14k gold is 58.5% pure gold, 41.5% alloy. It's soft relative to platinum.

Prong tips in bare 14k gold wear to flat points by year 5.

The fix: choose platinum prongs. Platinum is naturally white — no rhodium required. Platinum prong tips maintain their rounded grip profile for 15+ years.

The [Bold Diamond Eternity in Platinum at $5,750](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/bold-diamond-eternity-ring-in-platinum-7-mm-1-2-ct-tw-item-147445?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses full platinum construction. If budget limits you to 14k, get the prong tips re-tipped every 3–4 years. This costs $50–$150 per inspection.

