## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a men's diamond eternity band?

A men's diamond eternity band has diamonds set all the way around the full circumference of the band. This is different from a half-eternity, which has diamonds on the top 180° only. Eternity bands cannot be resized after purchase.

The setting type determines how long the ring holds up under daily wear. Channel, shared prong, and burnished each have different lifespans.

### What is the difference between a full eternity and half eternity band?

Full eternity: diamonds around 360°, cannot be resized, palm-side stones press against adjacent fingers during grip. Half eternity: diamonds on 180°, can be resized 1–2 sizes, plain metal on palm side.

For active men, half eternity is the more practical choice. See the Full vs Half Decision Matrix above for the complete comparison.

### Which setting type is most durable for a men's eternity band?

Burnished (flush) setting is the most durable. The diamond sits below the metal surface; no prong tips are exposed. Channel-set is second most durable — no prong tips, only channel walls that can undercut under heavy impact.

Shared prong with milgrain borders is third. Bare shared prong without milgrain is the least durable for daily active wear.

### Why do prong tips fail at year 5?

Shared prong tips wear flat from two directions. First: lateral impact thins the tip over time. Second: 14k white gold loses rhodium plating at the tip by year 2.

Once rhodium is gone, bare 14k gold is exposed.

14k gold is softer than platinum at the prong tip contact point. The tip wears flat; the stone seat loosens.

Platinum prong tips do not have this problem — they stay rounded for 15+ years.

### Can a men's diamond eternity band be resized?

Full eternity bands cannot be resized. Diamonds run the full circumference — there is no plain metal section to cut and adjust. If you gain or lose significant weight, you need a replacement band.

Half eternity bands and partial channel-set designs can be sized 1–2 sizes in either direction. Order a full eternity 1/4 to 1/2 size larger than your ring size. This allows for seasonal finger size variation.

### What diamond carat weight should a men's eternity band have?

For a 5–6mm band: 1/8–3/8 ct looks proportional. For 7mm: 3/8–1/2 ct. For 8mm+: 1/2 ct minimum to avoid the band looking sparse.

The [Milgrain Vertical Row at $2,880](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/milgrain-vertical-row-diamond-eternity-ring-in-14k-white-gold-7-mm-3-8-ct-tw-item-288422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) has 3/8 ct on a 7mm band — the standard proportion.

The [Milgrain Burnished Set at $3,590](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/milgrain-burnished-set-diamond-wedding-ring-in-14k-white-gold-5-mm-1-5-ct-tw-item-150698?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is 1.5 ct on a 5mm band — high density, burnished flush setting.

### What metal is best for a men's diamond eternity band?

14k rose gold is the best gold alloy. The copper content makes it harder (175 HV) and slows prong tip wear. Platinum is the best metal overall — no rhodium replating, prong tips last 15+ years.

Skip 18k: softer than 14k, costs 30–40% more. For the full metal comparison, see the [platinum vs. white gold guide](/round-diamond-platinum-vs-white-gold).

### How often does a men's diamond eternity band need maintenance?

Channel-set and burnished bands: clean every 6 months, no prong inspection needed. Shared prong bands: professional inspection every 12 months ($50–$100). Re-tip prong tips on 14k white gold every 3–5 years once rhodium has worn.

Replate rhodium on 14k white gold bands every 18–24 months. Platinum bands: clean every 6 months, no other maintenance.

### What is the cheapest men's diamond eternity band?

The [Slender Channel at $1,050](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/slender-channel-mens-diamond-wedding-band-in-14k-white-gold-550mm-by-james-allen-item-305347?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in 14k White Gold is the entry option — a 5.5mm channel-set band. The [Geometric Channel at $1,410](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/geometric-channel-mens-diamond-wedding-band-in-14k-white-gold-650mm-by-james-allen-item-305349?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the step up — 6.5mm with better diamond coverage. Both are channel-set: no prong tips, most durable setting type at the entry price.

### Can I stack a men's diamond eternity band with another ring?

Yes. Match the metal. White gold eternity next to white gold engagement ring. Yellow gold next to yellow gold.

Width matters too. Keep the eternity band and adjacent ring within 1–2mm of each other.

See the [men's emerald cut ring guide](/mens-emerald-cut-ring) for stacking examples. The [men's asscher cut ring guide](/mens-asscher-cut-ring) covers step-cut solitaire and eternity band combinations.

### What is the milgrain edge on a men's eternity band?

Milgrain is a row of tiny metal beads pressed along the top and bottom edges of the band. It serves two functions. First, it's decorative — an antique/vintage detail.

Second, it guards the shared prong tips from side-on lateral impact. The milgrain bead row absorbs impact before it reaches the prong tip.

The [Milgrain Vertical Row Eternity at $2,880](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/milgrain-vertical-row-diamond-eternity-ring-in-14k-white-gold-7-mm-3-8-ct-tw-item-288422?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses this design correctly.

### How is a burnished diamond setting different from a channel setting?

Channel-set: diamonds sit between two parallel walls; some stone height above the band surface. Burnished: diamonds sit below the band surface; metal is pressed over the girdle; stone flush with surface.

Burnished has zero exposed prong surface and cannot snag. The [Milgrain Burnished Set at $3,590](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/milgrain-burnished-set-diamond-wedding-ring-in-14k-white-gold-5-mm-1-5-ct-tw-item-150698?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the burnished option in this guide.

See the [men's diamond wedding band guide](/mens-diamond-wedding-band) for the full setting type comparison.

